In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a barren valley. Each village had a sacred spring that produced five vessels of water daily—barely enough to survive.

One day, a wanderer revealed an ancient secret: "If you pour your water into the valley between you, it will flow to your neighbor transformed—multiplied threefold by the minerals below."

The first village was afraid. "What if we give our water and receive nothing back? We'll perish."

But one brave elder said, "Let us trust." They poured three vessels into the valley. The water seeped through the earth and emerged ninefold at the other village's spring.

The neighboring village rejoiced at their sudden abundance. Their wise woman said, "This gift was born of trust. We must honor it." They returned five vessels.

From that day, the villages took turns sending water through the sacred ground. Some days they sent much, some days little. Sometimes they returned generously, sometimes cautiously. But they learned that the magic only worked through the courage to give and the wisdom to reciprocate.

The valley bloomed, and both villages prospered—not from the springs alone, but from the trust that flowed between them.
